==============================================================================
s05  Encoder-decoder, and the bottleneck
      a forward problem, which gating does nothing for
==============================================================================

5.1  Two networks, and the vector between them
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  eq:  encoder: s_1 .. s_L   ->   c = s_L   ->   decoder: y_1 .. y_M
       the source is read into a state; the target is written out of it

  This is the first architecture in the article that produces a
  sequence rather than consuming one, and it is what made machine
  translation a neural problem. The encoder and decoder are usually
  LSTMs, so both inherit S4's gradient path.

  Everything the decoder knows about the source arrives through c.
  c has the same width whether the source was four words or forty.

5.2  The bound, before any measurement
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  eq:  d * 32 bits  >=  L * log2(V) bits
       to distinguish every possible source of length L over a vocabulary of V, the state must have at least as many distinguishable configurations
  state width d  state bits (fp32)  bits per symbol  symbols distinguishable at best
  -------------  -----------------  ---------------  -------------------------------
              8                256             3.58                               71
             16                512             3.58                              143
             32              1,024             3.58                              286
             64              2,048             3.58                              571
  [PASS] the ceiling on what a fixed-width state can carry is linear in its width and independent of training
         a counting argument, so no encoder beats it: information that was never distinguishable cannot be recovered
  # this is an upper bound and a generous one -- it assumes every bit of every float is used perfectly. Real encoders fall short of it by a wide margin, which is why 5.3 measures rather than stopping here

5.3  What actually degrades
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Encode sequences of increasing length into a fixed-width vector, then
  try to read individual positions back out. The reader is given every
  advantage: it knows the encoder exactly and is told which position it
  wants.
  source length L  recovery rate  chance  usable?
  ---------------  -------------  ------  -------
                4         0.7958  0.0833  partial
                8           0.55  0.0833  partial
               16         0.3906  0.0833  partial
               32         0.2573  0.0833  no
               64         0.1799  0.0833  no
  [PASS] recall from a fixed-width context degrades as the source lengthens
         accuracy falls from 0.796 at L=4 to 0.180 at L=64, at a constant state width of 32
  [PASS] the failure is the width of the channel, not the length as such
         at L=32, widening the state from 16 to 128 raises accuracy from 0.182 to 0.437 -- the same sequence, a bigger pipe
  state width d  recovery rate at L=24  counting bound (symbols)
  -------------  ---------------------  ------------------------
              8                 0.1701                        71
             16                 0.2139                       143
             32                 0.2674                       286
             64                 0.4014                       571
            128                 0.5465                     1,143
  [PASS] accuracy rises with state width, as the bound predicts it must
         0.170 at d=8 rising to 0.546 at d=128
  # note how far the measured numbers sit below the counting bound. A 32-wide state could in principle distinguish hundreds of symbols and in practice recalls a fraction of twenty-four. The bound says what is impossible; it does not promise what is achievable

5.4  This is not the problem gating solved
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Worth being explicit, because the two failures are routinely run
  together and they have nothing in common.
                               vanishing gradient (S3, S4)        the bottleneck (here)
  ---------------------------  ---------------------------------  ----------------------------
  direction                    backward                           forward
  what fails                   the training signal cannot travel  the information cannot fit
  fixed by                     an additive path through time      not fixed by anything so far
  present in an LSTM encoder?  no -- gating repaired it           yes -- entirely unaffected
  [PASS] an encoder with a perfect gradient path still has a fixed-width context vector
         gating changed how the gradient travels between steps; it did not change how much a state can hold, and 5.2's bound never mentions gradients
  # the practical signature reported at the time was exactly this: translation quality that held up on short sentences and fell away on long ones, with no corresponding training instability. A gradient problem does not look like that

5.5  Where the fix has to come from
------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  There are only two ways to widen a channel that is too narrow: make
  it wider, or stop forcing everything through it.

  Making it wider fails on cost. The state's width is a fixed model
  parameter that has to be paid for on every sequence, short ones
  included, and 5.3 shows the returns are gradual while the cost is
  immediate. Sizing the state for the longest sentence you might ever
  see is the same mistake as sizing per-position weights for the
  longest sequence, which S2 already rejected.

  Which leaves the second option. The encoder already computed a state
  at every source position -- s_1 through s_L -- and the architecture
  throws all but the last one away. They are still there. The question
  is what to do with them.
  [PASS] the information the bottleneck destroys was computed and then discarded
         the encoder produces L states and the context vector keeps one; nothing needs to be recomputed to do better
  # that is the observation S6 is built on, and it is worth pausing on because it is the whole idea. Attention is not a new source of information. It is the decision to stop throwing away information the model already had.

s05 claims 6/6 passed in 7.28s
